-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 1: Cooling Tower Control Sequences for Building Automation
-- Category: Best Practices
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'Cooling Tower Control Sequences for Building Automation',
  'cooling-tower-control-sequences-bas',
  'Control sequences for cooling tower fan staging, condenser water temperature reset, basin heater lockout, and approach-based optimization per ASHRAE Guideline 36.',
  E'## Cooling Tower Control Sequences for Building Automation\n\nCooling towers reject heat from chilled-water plants by evaporating water across a fill medium while fans move ambient air through the tower. Proper control sequencing directly impacts chiller efficiency, energy consumption, and equipment longevity.\n\n## Condenser Water Temperature Control\n\n### Fixed Setpoint Control\n\nThe simplest approach holds a fixed condenser water supply temperature (CWST) setpoint, typically 80–85 °F (26.7–29.4 °C). Tower fans modulate to maintain CWST at the setpoint.\n\n| Parameter | Typical Value |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| CWST Setpoint | 80–85 °F |\n| Minimum CWST | Per chiller manufacturer (often 55–65 °F) |\n| Fan Control | Staging or VFD speed |\n\n### Approach-Based Control\n\nA more efficient method targets a fixed approach—the difference between CWST and outside-air wet-bulb temperature (OAWB):\n\n```\nCWST_Setpoint = OAWB + Approach\nTypical approach: 7–10 °F design, 5–7 °F achievable at part load\n```\n\nThe setpoint moves slowly as outdoor conditions change, while fans respond quickly to meet the current setpoint. This avoids over-cooling condenser water when ambient conditions already provide favorable temperatures.\n\n### Condenser Water Temperature Reset\n\nASHRAE Guideline 36 recommends resetting CWST based on plant operating conditions. The goal is to find the balance between reduced chiller lift (lower CWST saves compressor energy) and increased fan energy (more fan power needed to reach lower CWST).\n\n```\nReset Strategy:\n  When chiller load < 50%:\n    CWST_Setpoint = MAX(OAWB + Approach, Chiller_Min_ECWT)\n  When chiller load > 80%:\n    CWST_Setpoint = Design CWST (e.g., 85 °F)\n  Between 50–80%:\n    Linear interpolation\n```\n\nAlways respect the chiller manufacturer''s minimum entering condenser water temperature (ECWT) to avoid low-refrigerant-pressure faults.\n\n## Fan Staging Sequences\n\n### Constant-Speed Fan Staging\n\nFor towers with multiple constant-speed cells:\n\n```\nStage 1: Start one fan at low speed\nStage 2: Start second fan at low speed\nStage 3: Switch first fan to high speed\nStage 4: Switch second fan to high speed\n\nAlways start additional fans before switching to high speed\nto maximize total airflow per unit of energy.\n```\n\n### Variable-Speed Fan Control\n\nVFD-equipped towers should run all available fans at the same speed rather than running fewer fans at higher speed:\n\n```\nAll_Fan_Speed = PID(CWST, CWST_Setpoint)\nMinimum speed: Per manufacturer (typically 10–30%)\nMaximum speed: 100%\n\nFan power varies with the cube of speed (Fan Affinity Laws),\nso two fans at 50% use roughly 25% of the energy of one fan at 100%.\n```\n\n## Sump and Basin Heater Control\n\n### Basin Heater Enable\n\n```\nIf OAT < 40 °F AND Tower is OFF:\n  Enable basin heater\n  Setpoint: 40–45 °F\nIf OAT > 45 °F OR Tower is ON:\n  Disable basin heater\n```\n\n### Freeze Protection\n\n```\nIf CWST < 45 °F OR OAT < 35 °F:\n  Open condenser water bypass valve\n  Stage off tower fans\n  Enable basin heater if tower is idle\n```\n\n## Water Treatment and Blowdown\n\n- Maintain conductivity setpoint via automated blowdown valve\n- Monitor makeup water meter for leak detection\n- Chemical feed systems should interlock with condenser water pump status\n\n## Commissioning Checks\n\n| Test | Expected Result |\n|------|------------------|\n| Fan staging sequence | Fans stage per design, no simultaneous start |\n| CWST control | Temperature maintained within 2 °F of setpoint |\n| Basin heater lockout | Heater de-energizes when tower runs |\n| Minimum CWST limit | Setpoint does not drop below chiller minimum |\n| Fan VFD speed equalization | All fans run at equal speed |\n| Freeze protection | Fans stop, bypass opens below threshold |\n\n## Common Problems\n\n### Condenser Water Too Cold\n\n1. Verify minimum CWST limit is programmed\n2. Check chiller manufacturer''s minimum ECWT\n3. Add a three-way bypass valve if the system lacks one\n\n### Fan Cycling / Hunting\n\n1. Increase PID deadband (2–3 °F recommended)\n2. Add time delays between fan staging (5-minute minimum)\n3. Verify sensor location is in a representative location\n\n### Scale and Fouling\n\n1. Monitor approach trend over time—rising approach indicates fouling\n2. Inspect fill media and nozzles per manufacturer schedule\n3. Verify water treatment chemistry is within range\n\n## References\n\n- **ASHRAE Guideline 36-2021** — High-Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems, Chiller Plant section\n- **ASHRAE Handbook — HVAC Systems and Equipment (2024)** — Chapter 40: Cooling Towers\n- **Cooling Technology Institute (CTI)** — STD-201, Thermal Performance Testing\n- **Computrols** — "Cooling Tower Control Based on Approach" (computrols.com)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

