-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 2: BAS Grounding and Shielding Best Practices
-- Category: Best Practices
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'BAS Grounding and Shielding Best Practices',
  'bas-grounding-shielding-best-practices',
  'Field-tested grounding and shielding practices for BAS controller panels, communication trunks, and signal wiring to prevent noise, ground loops, and intermittent faults.',
  E'## BAS Grounding and Shielding Best Practices\n\nGrounding and shielding problems are among the most common causes of intermittent communication faults, phantom sensor readings, and unexplained controller resets in building automation systems. Getting grounding right during installation prevents weeks of troubleshooting later.\n\n## Grounding Fundamentals\n\n### Single-Point Ground Reference\n\nAll BAS equipment in an enclosure should reference a single ground bus bar. This prevents ground loops—circulating currents between two different ground potentials that inject noise into signal circuits.\n\n```\nPanel Ground Bus Bar\n  ├── Controller chassis ground\n  ├── Power supply ground\n  ├── Shield drain wires (landed here)\n  └── Equipment grounding conductor (to building ground per NEC 250)\n```\n\n### Conductor Sizing\n\nUse equipment grounding conductors sized per NEC Table 250.122, based on the rating of the overcurrent protective device. For BAS panel ground bus connections, a minimum of 8 AWG stranded copper or 1-inch copper braid is recommended between chassis, enclosures, and the central ground bus.\n\n## Shield Termination\n\n### The Single-End Rule\n\nFor most BAS signal cables (analog inputs, MS/TP trunks, sensor wiring):\n\n- **Ground the shield at one end only** — typically the controller/panel end\n- **Leave the shield floating at the field device end** (tape and insulate the drain wire)\n- This prevents ground loop currents from flowing through the shield\n\n```\nController End          Field Device End\n[Panel Ground] ◄── Shield ──── [Floating / Insulated]\n     │                              │\n  Drain wire                   Drain wire\n  to ground bus                taped off\n```\n\n### When to Ground Both Ends\n\nGround both ends only when:\n- The cable run is very short (under 10 ft) within the same panel\n- Both ends share the same ground reference with verified bonding\n- The manufacturer explicitly requires it\n\n### Maintaining Shield Continuity\n\nAt junction boxes and intermediate splice points:\n- Connect shields through from one segment to the next\n- Do **not** land intermediate shield connections to local ground\n- Breaking shield continuity creates antenna effects that amplify noise\n\n## Cable Routing\n\n### Separation Requirements\n\nMaintain physical separation between signal cables and power conductors:\n\n| Cable Type | Minimum Separation from Power |\n|------------|-------------------------------|\n| Analog sensor (4–20 mA, 0–10 V) | 12 inches |\n| BACnet MS/TP trunk | 12 inches |\n| Ethernet (BACnet/IP, FOX) | 6 inches (shielded Cat6) |\n| Low-voltage control (24 VAC) | 6 inches from line voltage |\n\n### Crossing Rules\n\nWhen signal and power cables must cross:\n- Cross at **90 degrees** to minimize electromagnetic coupling\n- Never run signal cables parallel to power conductors in the same tray\n- Use separate conduit for signal and power\n\n### VFD Proximity\n\nVariable frequency drives generate significant electromagnetic interference:\n- Route BAS signal cables at least 24 inches from VFD output cabling\n- Use shielded VFD output cable (per manufacturer recommendations)\n- Install line reactors or output filters when VFD noise is unavoidable\n\n## Power Supply Isolation\n\n### Transformer Grounding\n\n- Each controller panel should have a dedicated Class 2 transformer\n- Bond the transformer secondary ground to the panel ground bus\n- Do **not** share 24 VAC supplies across panels in different enclosures\n\n### Common 24 VAC Problems\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause |\n|---------|--------------|\n| Sensor reads erratically | Shared 24 VAC with a relay coil load |\n| Controller resets intermittently | Undersized transformer, voltage sag |\n| MS/TP communication drops near VFDs | Missing VFD output filter, poor routing |\n| Analog inputs show 60 Hz ripple | Ground loop or missing shield |\n\n## Commissioning Verification\n\n1. **Measure ground continuity** from panel ground bus to building ground (should be < 1 ohm)\n2. **Verify shield termination** at controller end only (measure with ohmmeter, shield disconnected)\n3. **Check for voltage between grounds** — more than 1 V AC between two panel grounds indicates a bonding problem\n4. **Inspect cable routing** for separation violations, especially near VFDs\n5. **Test analog inputs under load** — toggle nearby relays and check for noise on sensor readings\n\n## References\n\n- **NEC (NFPA 70) Article 250** — Grounding and Bonding\n- **NEC Table 250.122** — Equipment Grounding Conductor sizing\n- **Rockwell Automation Publication 1770-IN041** — Industrial Automation Wiring and Grounding Guidelines\n- **control.com** — "Fundamentals of Grounding in Automation Control Systems" (Technical Articles)\n- **Emerson** — Site Considerations for Equipment Installation, Grounding, and Wiring Manual',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

