-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 3: Control Valve Sizing and Selection for BAS
-- Category: Reference
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'Control Valve Sizing and Selection for BAS',
  'control-valve-sizing-selection-bas',
  'Reference guide for sizing HVAC control valves including Cv calculation, two-way vs. three-way selection, pressure drop guidelines, and actuator pairing.',
  E'## Control Valve Sizing and Selection for BAS\n\nControl valves regulate water flow through coils to modulate heating and cooling output. A properly sized valve provides stable modulating control. An oversized valve hunts, wastes energy, and shortens actuator life.\n\n## Cv: The Flow Coefficient\n\nCv is defined as the flow of water (in US gallons per minute at 60 °F) that passes through the valve with a 1 PSI pressure drop across it.\n\n### Sizing Formula\n\n```\nCv = Q × √(SG / ΔP)\n\nWhere:\n  Q  = Design flow rate (GPM)\n  SG = Specific gravity (1.0 for water, ~1.04 for glycol mix)\n  ΔP = Pressure drop across the valve at design flow (PSI)\n```\n\nSelect the catalog valve with a Cv equal to or slightly greater than the calculated value. Do **not** oversize.\n\n## Pressure Drop Guidelines\n\n### Valve Authority\n\nValve authority (N) is the ratio of the pressure drop across the fully open valve to the total pressure drop through the valve plus the controlled circuit:\n\n```\nN = ΔP_valve / (ΔP_valve + ΔP_coil)\n\nTarget: N ≥ 0.5 (50% authority)\nMinimum acceptable: N ≥ 0.3\n```\n\nHigher authority means more linear control. Low authority causes the valve to have almost no effect until nearly closed, then snap to full output.\n\n### Typical Pressure Drops\n\n| Component | Typical ΔP |\n|-----------|------------|\n| Heating coil | 2–5 PSI |\n| Cooling coil | 3–8 PSI |\n| Control valve (rule of thumb) | 3–5 PSI |\n| Control valve (best practice) | Equal to or greater than coil ΔP |\n\nIf the coil ΔP is unknown, the industry standard is to size the valve for a 3–5 PSI drop.\n\n## Two-Way vs. Three-Way Valves\n\n### Two-Way Valves\n\nRegulate flow by throttling—the valve opens or closes to control the amount of water through the coil.\n\n**Use when:**\n- Variable-flow systems with VFD-equipped pumps\n- Systems designed for ΔP-based pump speed control\n- Energy efficiency is a priority (reduced pump energy at part load)\n\n**Sizing note:** The valve sees the full system differential pressure when nearly closed. Ensure the actuator close-off rating exceeds the maximum system ΔP.\n\n### Three-Way Valves\n\nMix or divert flow—total flow through the valve body remains roughly constant.\n\n**Use when:**\n- Constant-flow systems (primary loop, process loads)\n- Chiller or boiler minimum flow requirements must be maintained\n- A bypass is needed to prevent coil freezing or thermal shock\n\n**Mixing vs. diverting:**\n\n| Configuration | Common Port | Application |\n|---------------|-------------|-------------|\n| Mixing | Outlet | Blending hot and cold return |\n| Diverting | Inlet | Splitting supply to coil and bypass |\n\nA properly sized three-way valve is typically one or two pipe sizes smaller than the line size.\n\n## Valve Characteristics\n\n| Characteristic | Flow Behavior | Best For |\n|----------------|--------------|----------|\n| Equal percentage | Small change at low lift, large at high lift | Most HVAC coils (compensates for coil nonlinearity) |\n| Linear | Proportional change across full stroke | Heat exchangers with linear response |\n| Quick-opening | Large change at low lift | On/off service only |\n\nFor HVAC coil control, **equal percentage** is the default selection.\n\n## Actuator Selection\n\n### Actuator Sizing\n\n```\nRequired close-off force > Maximum system ΔP × Valve area\n```\n\n| Actuator Type | Typical Application |\n|---------------|--------------------|\n| Floating (tri-state) | On/off and slow-modulating two-position |\n| Proportional (0–10 V or 4–20 mA) | Modulating control with BAS output |\n| Spring-return | Fail-safe position required (NC or NO) |\n\n### Fail Position\n\n| Application | Recommended Fail Position |\n|-------------|---------------------------|\n| Heating coil | Fail open (NC valve, spring return open) |\n| Cooling coil | Fail closed (NO valve, spring return closed) |\n| Chilled water in freeze-prone area | Fail open to prevent coil freeze |\n\n## Common Sizing Mistakes\n\n1. **Line-sizing the valve** — selecting the same size as the pipe rather than calculating Cv\n2. **Ignoring authority** — oversized valve with low ΔP gives poor control\n3. **Wrong close-off rating** — actuator cannot hold valve shut at system pressure\n4. **Using linear characteristic on coils** — leads to nonlinear heat output\n5. **Selecting three-way for variable flow** — wastes pump energy\n\n## Quick Sizing Table\n\n| Flow (GPM) | ΔP = 3 PSI | ΔP = 5 PSI |\n|------------|------------|------------|\n| 5 | Cv = 2.9 | Cv = 2.2 |\n| 10 | Cv = 5.8 | Cv = 4.5 |\n| 25 | Cv = 14.4 | Cv = 11.2 |\n| 50 | Cv = 28.9 | Cv = 22.4 |\n| 100 | Cv = 57.7 | Cv = 44.7 |\n\n## References\n\n- **ASHRAE Handbook — HVAC Systems and Equipment (2024)** — Chapter 48: Valves\n- **Belimo** — "Electronic Valve Sizing and Selection" Technical Document (belimo.com)\n- **Schneider Electric** — Control Valve Sizing Application Note, Publication F-13755\n- **Griswold Controls** — Actuated Control Valves Sizing Guide, Publication F-4334\n- **AutomatedBuildings.com** — "Control Valves Sizing & Selection" by Frank Calabrese (Feb 2009)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

