-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 4: Building Pressurization Control for BAS
-- Category: Best Practices
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'Building Pressurization Control for BAS',
  'building-pressurization-control-bas',
  'Control sequences for maintaining positive building pressure, relief fan control, stairwell pressurization, and differential pressure sensor placement per ASHRAE and IBC requirements.',
  E'## Building Pressurization Control for BAS\n\nMaintaining proper building pressure prevents uncontrolled infiltration, stack effect issues, difficulty opening doors, and is critical for smoke control compliance. The BAS plays a central role in monitoring and controlling building pressure.\n\n## Why Building Pressure Matters\n\n- **Positive pressure** prevents humid outdoor air infiltration, reducing latent loads and mold risk\n- **Excessive positive pressure** makes exterior doors hard to open (ADA and IBC concern)\n- **Negative pressure** causes drafts, uncontrolled outdoor air entry, and comfort complaints\n- **Stairwell pressurization** is a life safety requirement for smoke control in many jurisdictions\n\n## Pressure Measurement\n\n### Differential Pressure Sensors\n\nBuilding pressure is measured as the difference between interior space static pressure and outside-air static pressure (building ΔP).\n\n| Parameter | Typical Value |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| Target building ΔP | +0.03 to +0.05 in. w.c. |\n| Maximum building ΔP | +0.10 in. w.c. (above this, doors become hard to open) |\n| Sensor range | -0.25 to +0.25 in. w.c. |\n| Sensor accuracy | ±0.01 in. w.c. minimum |\n\n### Sensor Placement\n\n- **Indoor reference:** Interior corridor or lobby, away from HVAC supply diffusers, at 4–5 ft height\n- **Outdoor reference:** Exterior port on a wind-sheltered wall, or use a static pressure averaging array\n- Avoid placing outdoor reference taps on windward walls or near exhaust discharges\n\n## Control Sequences\n\n### Return/Relief Fan Control\n\nIn systems with supply and return/relief fans:\n\n```\nRelief_Fan_Speed = PID(Building_DP, Building_DP_Setpoint)\n\nSetpoint: +0.05 in. w.c. (adjustable)\nPID Output: Modulates relief fan VFD speed\nMinimum speed: Per fan curve (typically 20–30%)\n```\n\nAlternative approach using return fan tracking:\n\n```\nReturn_Fan_Speed = Supply_Fan_Speed - Offset\n\nOffset accounts for:\n  - Exhaust fan airflow\n  - Building envelope leakage\n  - Desired pressurization\n```\n\n### Relief Damper Control\n\nFor systems using barometric or motorized relief dampers:\n\n```\nRelief_Damper_Position = PID(Building_DP, Building_DP_Setpoint)\n\nDamper opens as building pressure exceeds setpoint\nDamper closes to maintain minimum positive pressure\n```\n\n### Economizer Impact\n\nWhen the economizer is at 100% outdoor air:\n- Building pressure increases significantly\n- Relief path must be adequately sized\n- Control must prevent over-pressurization\n\n```\nIf OA_Damper > 80%:\n  Increase relief fan speed or open relief damper\n  Monitor building ΔP continuously\n  Alarm if ΔP > +0.10 in. w.c.\n```\n\n## Stairwell Pressurization\n\n### Code Requirements\n\nPer IBC Section 909.20 and NFPA 92:\n\n| Parameter | Requirement |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| Minimum ΔP (all doors closed) | +0.10 in. w.c. |\n| Maximum ΔP (all doors closed) | +0.35 in. w.c. |\n| Door opening force | ≤ 30 lbf (IBC 1010.1.3) |\n| Measurement reference | Stairwell relative to adjacent occupied floor |\n\n### Control Strategy\n\n```\nStairwell_Fan_Speed = PID(Stairwell_DP, Stairwell_DP_Setpoint)\n\nSetpoint: +0.15 to +0.20 in. w.c.\nBypass damper: Modulates to prevent over-pressurization\n  when all doors are closed\n```\n\n### Sensor Locations\n\n- Install ΔP sensors at minimum three floors: top, middle, bottom\n- Use the lowest reading for fan speed control (worst case)\n- Alarm on any sensor reading below minimum\n\n## BAS Integration Points\n\n| Point | Type | Description |\n|-------|------|-------------|\n| Building ΔP | AI | Differential pressure sensor |\n| Relief fan speed | AO | VFD speed command |\n| Relief fan status | DI | Fan run status |\n| Relief damper position | AO | Damper command |\n| Stairwell ΔP (per zone) | AI | Stairwell differential pressure |\n| Stairwell fan speed | AO | Pressurization fan VFD command |\n| Smoke mode status | DI | Fire alarm system interlock |\n\n## Commissioning Checks\n\n| Test | Expected Result |\n|------|------------------|\n| All AHUs off, building sealed | Measure baseline leakage |\n| Supply fan on, relief off | Building ΔP increases (confirms positive flow) |\n| Economizer at 100% OA | Relief fan/damper modulates to maintain setpoint |\n| All stairwell doors closed | ΔP within 0.10–0.35 in. w.c. |\n| One stairwell door open | ΔP may drop; fan speed increases |\n| Door opening force | ≤ 30 lbf at any stairwell door |\n\n## Common Problems\n\n### Building Pressure Swings\n\n1. Check outdoor reference port for wind exposure\n2. Slow down PID response (integral-dominant tuning)\n3. Verify relief path capacity is adequate for economizer mode\n\n### Stairwell Over-Pressurization\n\n1. Verify bypass damper is operational\n2. Check ΔP sensor calibration\n3. Confirm fan speed is not locked at 100%\n\n## References\n\n- **ASHRAE Handbook — HVAC Applications (2023)** — Chapter 53: Smoke Management\n- **IBC (International Building Code)** — Section 909.20: Stairway Pressurization Systems\n- **NFPA 92 (2024)** — Standard for Smoke Control Systems\n- **HPAC Engineering** — "Pressurization Control in Large Commercial Buildings" (hpac.com)\n- **CED Engineering** — "Stairwell Pressurization Systems" course reference (cedengineering.com)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

