-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 5: Fan Coil Unit (FCU) Control Sequences
-- Category: Reference
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'Fan Coil Unit (FCU) Control Sequences',
  'fan-coil-unit-fcu-control-sequences',
  'Control sequences for 2-pipe, 4-pipe, and changeover fan coil unit systems including valve staging, fan speed control, and BAS integration points.',
  E'## Fan Coil Unit (FCU) Control Sequences\n\nFan coil units are decentralized terminal units that condition individual zones using a fan and one or more hydronic coils. FCU control sequences vary significantly depending on the piping configuration (2-pipe vs. 4-pipe) and whether changeover is required.\n\n## FCU Piping Configurations\n\n### 2-Pipe System\n\nA single coil serves both heating and cooling. The building switches between hot water and chilled water seasonally or based on outdoor conditions.\n\n```\nSupply ──► [Single Coil] ──► Return\n           (HW or CHW)\n```\n\n**Advantages:** Lower first cost, simpler piping\n**Disadvantages:** Cannot simultaneously heat and cool different zones; changeover delay causes comfort gaps\n\n### 4-Pipe System\n\nSeparate heating and cooling coils with independent supply and return piping.\n\n```\nCHW Supply ──► [Cooling Coil] ──► CHW Return\nHW Supply  ──► [Heating Coil] ──► HW Return\n```\n\n**Advantages:** Simultaneous heating and cooling available; no changeover required; better zone-level comfort\n**Disadvantages:** Higher installation cost; more piping and valves\n\n## 2-Pipe Control Sequence\n\n### With Manual/Seasonal Changeover\n\n```\nMode determined by building operator or schedule:\n\nCooling Mode:\n  Valve_Position = PID(Zone_Temp, Cooling_Setpoint)\n  Fan_Speed = Based on load or occupancy\n\nHeating Mode:\n  Valve_Position = PID(Zone_Temp, Heating_Setpoint)\n  Fan_Speed = Based on load or occupancy\n```\n\n### With Automatic Changeover\n\nA changeover sensor (typically a pipe-mount temperature sensor on the supply line) determines the current mode:\n\n```\nIf Supply_Water_Temp < Changeover_Threshold (e.g., 65 °F):\n  Mode = Cooling\n  Valve = PID(Zone_Temp, Cooling_Setpoint)\nElse If Supply_Water_Temp > Changeover_Threshold (e.g., 90 °F):\n  Mode = Heating\n  Valve = PID(Zone_Temp, Heating_Setpoint)\nElse:\n  Mode = Deadband (valve closed)\n```\n\nThe changeover threshold prevents short-cycling between modes and should include a deadband of at least 5 °F.\n\n## 4-Pipe Control Sequence\n\n### Heating/Cooling Staging\n\n```\nDeadband: 2–4 °F between heating and cooling setpoints\n\nIf Zone_Temp > Cooling_Setpoint:\n  CHW_Valve = PID(Zone_Temp, Cooling_Setpoint)\n  HW_Valve = Closed\n\nIf Zone_Temp < Heating_Setpoint:\n  HW_Valve = PID(Zone_Temp, Heating_Setpoint)\n  CHW_Valve = Closed\n\nIf Heating_Setpoint < Zone_Temp < Cooling_Setpoint:\n  Both valves closed (deadband - no energy use)\n```\n\nInterlocking ensures both valves never open simultaneously, preventing energy waste from simultaneous heating and cooling.\n\n## Fan Speed Control\n\n### Multi-Speed Fan\n\nTypical residential/light commercial FCUs have 3 fan speeds:\n\n```\nAuto mode (load-based):\n  If |Zone_Temp - Setpoint| > 3 °F: High speed\n  If |Zone_Temp - Setpoint| > 1.5 °F: Medium speed\n  If |Zone_Temp - Setpoint| ≤ 1.5 °F: Low speed\n  If in deadband: Fan off or low speed (configurable)\n```\n\n### ECM/Variable-Speed Fan\n\n```\nFan_Speed = PID(Zone_Temp, Active_Setpoint)\nMinimum speed: 20–30% (for ventilation if required)\nMaximum speed: 100%\n```\n\n## Occupancy-Based Control\n\n```\nOccupied Mode:\n  Cooling setpoint: 74 °F\n  Heating setpoint: 70 °F\n  Fan: Auto or continuous low\n\nUnoccupied Mode:\n  Cooling setpoint: 85 °F (setback)\n  Heating setpoint: 60 °F (setback)\n  Fan: Off or cycles on valve call\n\nOccupancy determined by:\n  - BAS schedule\n  - Local occupancy sensor\n  - BACnet occupancy command from supervisory controller\n```\n\n## BAS Integration Points\n\n| Point | Type | Description |\n|-------|------|-------------|\n| Zone temperature | AI | Room temperature sensor |\n| Zone setpoint | AO/BV | Active heating or cooling setpoint |\n| CHW valve command | AO | Cooling valve position (4-pipe) |\n| HW valve command | AO | Heating valve position (4-pipe) |\n| Valve command | AO | Single valve position (2-pipe) |\n| Fan speed command | AO/MO | Fan speed (variable or multi-speed) |\n| Occupancy status | DI/BV | Occupied/unoccupied from sensor or schedule |\n| Supply water temp | AI | Changeover sensor (2-pipe systems) |\n| Fan status | DI | Fan run confirmation |\n\n## Commissioning Checks\n\n| Test | Expected Result |\n|------|------------------|\n| Cooling call | CHW valve opens, fan runs, discharge air cools |\n| Heating call | HW valve opens, fan runs, discharge air heats |\n| Deadband (4-pipe) | Both valves closed, no energy use |\n| Changeover (2-pipe) | Correct mode based on supply water temp |\n| Occupancy override | Setpoints shift to unoccupied values |\n| Fan speed staging | Speed increases with load, decreases as satisfied |\n| Valve interlock (4-pipe) | Both valves never open simultaneously |\n\n## Common Problems\n\n### Simultaneous Heating and Cooling (4-Pipe)\n\n1. Verify valve interlock logic is programmed\n2. Increase deadband between heating and cooling setpoints\n3. Check for stuck or leaking valves (physically verify)\n\n### 2-Pipe Changeover Hunting\n\n1. Increase changeover deadband to at least 5 °F\n2. Add minimum time in mode (30–60 minutes)\n3. Verify changeover sensor location and calibration\n\n### Fan Runs But No Heating/Cooling\n\n1. Verify valve is receiving signal and actuator is functional\n2. Check water flow — isolation valves may be closed\n3. Confirm correct mode for 2-pipe systems\n\n## References\n\n- **ASHRAE Handbook — HVAC Systems and Equipment (2024)** — Chapter 6: Panel Heating and Cooling, and Chapter 5: In-Room Terminal Systems\n- **CIBSE Journal** — "Module 101: The Evolution of Fan Coils for Efficient Conditioning of Room Air" (CPD Module 2016-10)\n- **Neptronic** — "2 Pipe vs 4 Pipe Fan Coil" Technical Reference (neptronic.com)\n- **Constructandcommission.com** — "Fan Coil Units: What, Where & How"',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

