-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 6: BAS Scheduling Best Practices: Optimal Start/Stop
--            and Night Setback
-- Category: Best Practices
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'BAS Scheduling Best Practices: Optimal Start/Stop and Night Setback',
  'bas-scheduling-optimal-start-stop-night-setback',
  'Best practices for BAS time schedules, optimal start/stop algorithms, night setback/setup temperatures, and holiday scheduling per ASHRAE 90.1 and Guideline 36.',
  E'## BAS Scheduling Best Practices: Optimal Start/Stop and Night Setback\n\nScheduling is one of the simplest and most impactful energy conservation measures in building automation. A well-configured schedule with optimal start/stop and proper setback temperatures can reduce HVAC energy use by 10–30% with no hardware changes.\n\n## Schedule Fundamentals\n\n### Occupied/Unoccupied Modes\n\nEvery zone should operate in one of two primary modes:\n\n| Mode | Heating SP | Cooling SP | Ventilation | Fan |\n|------|-----------|-----------|-------------|-----|\n| Occupied | 70–72 °F | 74–76 °F | Per ASHRAE 62.1 | On |\n| Unoccupied | 55–60 °F | 85–90 °F | Off | Off or cycles |\n\n### ASHRAE 90.1 Requirements\n\nASHRAE 90.1-2022 Section 6.4.3.3.2 requires:\n\n- Automatic setback/setup capability for all HVAC zones\n- Heating setback: at least 10 °F below occupied heating setpoint\n- Cooling setup: at least 5 °F above occupied cooling setpoint\n- Optimum start controls for systems serving areas greater than 25,000 sq ft\n\n## Night Setback and Setup\n\n### Heating Setback\n\nDuring unoccupied periods, the heating setpoint drops to save energy while preventing freeze damage:\n\n```\nUnoccupied_Heating_SP = Occupied_Heating_SP - Setback_Offset\n\nTypical setback: 10–15 °F\nExample: 72 °F occupied → 58 °F unoccupied\n\nFreeze protection override:\n  If ANY zone temp < 40 °F:\n    Start heating regardless of schedule\n    Alarm to operator\n```\n\n### Cooling Setup\n\nDuring unoccupied periods, the cooling setpoint rises (or cooling is disabled):\n\n```\nUnoccupied_Cooling_SP = Occupied_Cooling_SP + Setup_Offset\n\nTypical setup: 5–15 °F\nExample: 74 °F occupied → 85 °F unoccupied\n\nHigh-limit protection:\n  If ANY zone temp > 95 °F:\n    Start cooling regardless of schedule\n    (Protects equipment and finishes)\n```\n\n## Optimal Start\n\n### Purpose\n\nOptimal start calculates the latest possible time to start HVAC equipment so that zone temperatures reach occupied setpoints exactly at the scheduled occupancy time. This avoids wasting energy by starting equipment too early.\n\n### Algorithm Approach\n\nASHRAE Guideline 36 describes an adaptive optimal start algorithm:\n\n```\nPre-Start_Time = f(ΔT, Thermal_Mass, OAT, Historical_Recovery)\n\nWhere:\n  ΔT = Current zone temp - Occupied setpoint\n  Thermal_Mass = Building construction factor (learned)\n  OAT = Current outdoor air temperature\n  Historical_Recovery = Learned recovery rate from previous days\n```\n\n### Implementation\n\n```\nStep 1: At scheduled occupancy time minus maximum lead time\n        (e.g., 4 hours), begin evaluating zone temps\n\nStep 2: Calculate required recovery time based on:\n        - Temperature difference (zone vs. setpoint)\n        - Outdoor conditions\n        - Historical recovery rate (adaptive)\n\nStep 3: Start equipment at:\n        Occupancy_Time - Calculated_Recovery_Time\n\nStep 4: After each day, update the adaptive model:\n        - If zone reached setpoint early: reduce lead time\n        - If zone was late: increase lead time\n```\n\n### Typical Lead Times\n\n| Building Type | Heating Lead | Cooling Lead |\n|---------------|-------------|---------------|\n| Light construction (metal/glass) | 30–90 min | 15–60 min |\n| Medium construction (concrete/brick) | 60–180 min | 30–120 min |\n| Heavy construction (stone/mass) | 120–240 min | 60–180 min |\n\n## Optimal Stop\n\n### Purpose\n\nOptimal stop shuts down HVAC equipment before the end of occupancy, using the building''s thermal mass to maintain comfort through the remainder of the occupied period.\n\n```\nPre-Stop_Time = f(Thermal_Mass, Remaining_Occupied_Hours, OAT)\n\nTypical: Stop equipment 30–60 minutes before unoccupied\n         (building thermal mass maintains comfort)\n```\n\n### Constraints\n\n- Do not apply optimal stop if the remaining occupied time is less than the estimated coast time\n- Monitor zone temperatures during coast and restart if any zone drifts outside comfort limits\n- Ventilation (outdoor air) must continue per ASHRAE 62.1 until the last occupant leaves\n\n## Holiday and Exception Scheduling\n\n### Holiday Calendars\n\n```\nPriority (highest to lowest):\n  1. Holiday schedule (override)\n  2. Exception schedule (special event)\n  3. Weekly recurring schedule\n  4. Default schedule\n```\n\n### Best Practices\n\n- Program holidays at least one year in advance\n- Include the day before and after major holidays (reduced occupancy)\n- Create a "special event" exception for after-hours use\n- Require an automatic revert — never rely on operators to manually cancel overrides\n\n## Schedule Verification\n\n| Check | Method |\n|-------|--------|\n| Equipment starts at correct time | Trend logs on fan/pump start commands |\n| Optimal start adapts to conditions | Compare start times across varying OAT days |\n| Setback temperatures are correct | Trend zone temps during unoccupied hours |\n| Holiday schedule works | Verify equipment stays off on known holidays |\n| Override auto-expiration | Apply a temporary override and verify it expires |\n| Freeze protection fires | Force a low zone temp and verify heating starts |\n\n## Energy Savings Estimates\n\n| Measure | Typical Savings |\n|---------|------------------|\n| Nighttime setback (heating) | 5–15% heating energy |\n| Nighttime setup (cooling) | 3–10% cooling energy |\n| Optimal start | 3–8% overall HVAC energy |\n| Optimal stop | 1–3% overall HVAC energy |\n| Holiday scheduling | 2–5% annual energy |\n\n## References\n\n- **ASHRAE Standard 90.1-2022** — Section 6.4.3.3.2: Setback and Optimum Start Controls\n- **ASHRAE Guideline 36-2021** — Optimal Start Sequences, Section 5.18\n- **ASHRAE Standard 62.1-2022** — Ventilation for Acceptable Indoor Air Quality\n- **Whole Building Design Guide (WBDG)** — "Optimal Scheduling" (wbdg.org)\n- **Honeywell** — "ASHRAE Guideline 36: Your Guide to the New Guideline" Technical Brief (buildings.honeywell.com)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

