-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 7: Pneumatic-to-DDC Retrofit Planning Guide
-- Category: How-To
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'how-to'),
  'Pneumatic-to-DDC Retrofit Planning Guide',
  'pneumatic-to-ddc-retrofit-planning-guide',
  'Step-by-step planning guide for converting pneumatic HVAC controls to DDC, covering survey, phasing, transducer strategies, and hybrid approaches.',
  E'## Pneumatic-to-DDC Retrofit Planning Guide\n\nConverting from pneumatic controls to Direct Digital Control (DDC) is one of the most common building automation retrofit projects. Pneumatic systems—which use compressed air at 3–15 PSI to position valves, dampers, and actuators—are still widespread in buildings constructed before the mid-1990s, but replacement parts are increasingly scarce and the systems cannot provide the monitoring, scheduling, and energy optimization capabilities of modern DDC.\n\n## Assessing the Existing System\n\n### Pre-Survey Checklist\n\nBefore starting design:\n\n1. **Locate the air compressor(s)** — Note capacity, age, condition, and whether dedicated to controls or shared\n2. **Trace main air supply lines** — Map the distribution from compressor to mechanical rooms and zones\n3. **Inventory pneumatic devices:**\n   - Thermostats (room, duct, insertion)\n   - Receiver-controllers\n   - Valve and damper actuators (note size, spring range, action)\n   - Pneumatic-electric (P/E) switches\n   - Transducers already in place\n4. **Document existing sequences** — Many pneumatic systems have no written sequence of operations; trace the tubing to understand control logic\n5. **Assess mechanical equipment condition** — There is no benefit in connecting new DDC to equipment nearing end of life\n\n### What to Keep vs. Replace\n\n| Component | Typical Decision |\n|-----------|------------------|\n| Room thermostats | Replace with DDC sensors |\n| Receiver-controllers | Replace with DDC controllers |\n| Valve actuators (< 15 years old) | Consider keeping with E/P transducer |\n| Valve actuators (> 15 years old) | Replace with electronic actuators |\n| Damper actuators (large) | Consider keeping with E/P transducer |\n| Damper actuators (small/corroded) | Replace with electronic actuators |\n| Control air compressor | Decommission if full DDC conversion |\n| Main air piping | Abandon in place or remove |\n\n## Conversion Strategies\n\n### Full Replacement\n\nReplace all pneumatic devices with electronic DDC equivalents:\n\n- DDC controllers with I/O boards\n- Electronic valve actuators (0–10 V or floating)\n- Electronic damper actuators\n- Temperature, pressure, and humidity sensors (4–20 mA, 0–10 V, or digital)\n\n**Pros:** Clean installation, full BAS capability, no legacy dependencies\n**Cons:** Highest cost, longest downtime, most disruptive\n\n### Hybrid with Transducers\n\nKeep existing pneumatic actuators and add transducers to interface with the new DDC system:\n\n**Electric-to-Pneumatic (E/P) Transducers:**\n- Convert DDC output signal (0–10 V or 4–20 mA) to pneumatic pressure (3–15 PSI)\n- Allows the DDC controller to position existing pneumatic valve and damper actuators\n- Requires the control air compressor to remain operational\n\n**Pneumatic-to-Electric (P/E) Transducers:**\n- Convert pneumatic sensor signals (3–15 PSI) to electrical signals readable by the DDC controller\n- Used when keeping pneumatic sensors temporarily during a phased conversion\n\n```\n[DDC Controller] ──0-10V──► [E/P Transducer] ──3-15 PSI──► [Pneumatic Actuator]\n\n[Pneumatic Sensor] ──3-15 PSI──► [P/E Transducer] ──4-20mA──► [DDC Controller]\n```\n\n**Pros:** Lower cost, less disruption, phases conversion over time\n**Cons:** Air compressor must stay running, transducers add failure points, limited BAS features for legacy devices\n\n### Wireless Pneumatic Thermostat (WPT) Overlay\n\nWireless pneumatic thermostats mount over existing pneumatic thermostats and add DDC-like functionality (scheduling, setpoint control, occupancy sensing) while still using the pneumatic actuator infrastructure:\n\n**Pros:** Lowest cost, fastest installation (often under 30 minutes per zone), no wiring\n**Cons:** Limited to thermostat-level control, does not provide full DDC I/O, compressor must remain\n\n## Phasing the Project\n\n### Recommended Phasing Order\n\n```\nPhase 1: Central plant (chillers, boilers, pumps)\n  - Highest energy impact\n  - Fewest zones, concentrated equipment\n  - Often already partially DDC\n\nPhase 2: Air handling units\n  - Economizer, supply fan, mixed air control\n  - Damper and valve actuator replacement\n  - Duct sensors and safeties\n\nPhase 3: Terminal units (VAV, FCU, unit ventilators)\n  - Highest quantity, most labor\n  - Zone-level comfort impact\n  - Can use E/P transducers to phase over time\n\nPhase 4: Decommission compressor and air piping\n  - Only after all pneumatic devices are removed\n  - Remove or cap tubing to prevent confusion\n```\n\n## Wiring and Infrastructure\n\n### New Wiring Requirements\n\n| Circuit Type | Typical Cable | Notes |\n|--------------|--------------|-------|\n| Sensor (analog) | 18/2 shielded plenum-rated | One pair per sensor |\n| Actuator (0–10 V) | 18/2 or 18/3 plenum-rated | Check actuator requirements |\n| Actuator (floating) | 18/3 plenum-rated | Open/close/common |\n| BACnet MS/TP | 18/2 shielded + ground, plenum-rated | Daisy chain, 4000 ft max |\n| Power (24 VAC) | 18/2 per transformer secondary | Dedicated transformer per panel |\n\n### Panel and Controller Placement\n\n- Locate DDC panels in mechanical rooms near the equipment they serve\n- Plan I/O count with 10–15% spare capacity for future additions\n- Verify 120 VAC power availability for controller panels and transformers\n\n## Cost Considerations\n\n| Approach | Relative Cost per Zone |\n|----------|------------------------|\n| Full DDC replacement | $$$$$ |\n| Hybrid with E/P transducers | $$$$ |\n| WPT overlay | $$ |\n| No action (maintain pneumatics) | $ (but rising parts costs) |\n\nFull DDC typically costs $2,000–$5,000 per zone depending on scope, though costs vary significantly by region, system complexity, and whether the mechanical equipment is also being replaced.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Not surveying actuator spring ranges** — If you keep a pneumatic actuator, the E/P transducer must match the actuator''s spring range (e.g., 3–8 PSI, 8–13 PSI)\n2. **Undersizing the control air compressor for hybrid** — If you remove half the pneumatic devices, the compressor may short-cycle; consider adding a receiver tank\n3. **Forgetting to update sequences** — Don''t just replicate the pneumatic sequence in DDC; take the opportunity to implement modern sequences (ASHRAE Guideline 36)\n4. **No documentation of existing system** — Trace and document every pneumatic circuit before removal\n5. **Phasing without a network plan** — DDC controllers need a communication backbone (MS/TP or IP); plan the network before installing controllers\n\n## References\n\n- **ASHRAE Guideline 36-2021** — High-Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems\n- **RasMech** — "Pneumatic to DDC Conversion: A Facility Manager''s Guide" (rasmech.com)\n- **HPAC Engineering** — "When Good Systems Have Obsolete Controls" (hpac.com)\n- **HVACProSales** — "Pneumatic HVAC Controls: Operation, Maintenance, and Conversion to DDC" (hvacprosales.com)\n- **Facility Executive** — "Wireless Thermostat Conversion" (facilityexecutive.com)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

